{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Enabled the demotion variant now that an apply-side rejection is counted rather than fatal, and the sweep produced the exact shape @joint_apply_order was asking about, through the repository's own producers. Draft-side the revision completes and reports invalid_change_applies=3 with all three reasons 'config is already joint', against 245 configuration changes, 446 restarts, 481 elections, 51 unapplied-change check points, stalled_states=0 and violations=0 - a node was handed a committed joint-entering configuration change while its own tracker was joint, three times in one run, with configuration events aimed at serving members (demote/promote) as well as the joining node, joint transitions, restarts and the liveness witness all present. That shows no safety violation (the four invariants held) but it does show the panic path is reached by permitted event sequences, which contradicts what I concluded two turns ago: the propose-time alreadyPending/alreadyJoint rewrite bounds what the leader appends given its own applied configuration, yet it does not exclude a joint-entering change reaching a node whose own configuration is joint, so the residual case is not limited to DisableConfChangeValidation. I corrected that knowledge entry's premise accordingly (changed_premise, narrowed statement, conditions and knowledge link to the campaign-guard entry that shares the applied/committed gap) and requested isolated execution of these bytes so the three occurrences and their reasons are receipt-backed. Stated honestly in the request: the instrument records occurrences and messages, not the entry order that produced them, and the target list excludes the current leader by harness choice; if the receipt reproduces the three rejections, the next step is a deterministic prefix that preserves the rejecting node's entry order, which is what would settle whether this is a target-reachable availability hazard or a harness artefact.\"}"}}
